Two slots: a floor that cannot be taken from you, and a switch that says so

`sw.js` kept the shell in one cache, keyed by a `VERSION` constant, holding the
shell *and* every runtime response — told apart only by whether a path happened
to be in the shell list. That made "go forward" inexpressible, and it shipped a
real lockout.

## The lockout, which was live

The old `activate()` deleted every shell cache whose key differed from `VERSION`
the moment a new worker took over. Kill the network mid-install and the holder
was left with a half-filled new cache and **no floor** — the system locking
someone out of their own copy, which is the one outcome this design exists to
prevent.

Deletion is now a **consequence of a proven floor** (`retireLegacy()`, gated on
the floor being whole, reachable from both activate and promote) and never of
activation.

**Verified in Chromium against the real failure:** an upgrade from the one-cache
worker on a crippled network **rescued 46 of 49 files** out of the old cache into
the floor and **kept the old cache** because the floor was still three short. A
later refresh completed the floor, and only then was the old cache retired.

## The two slots

- **`anecdote-held`** — the floor. Proven-whole, boots with the origin dead,
  moves only on a deliberate promote.
- **`anecdote-rolling`** — what you run. Always accumulating, allowed to be
  partial.
- **`anecdote-runtime`** — everything not shell, network-first as before.

Slot names are **not versioned**. A cache key carrying the version is exactly
what stranded installs, so a generation change is a merge rather than a discard.

**Promotion is a merge, never a swap:** rolling's bytes where it has them, held's
where it does not. The floor is whole by construction and cannot acquire a hole —
which is why completeness is a number the control page *shows* rather than a veto
anywhere in the code.

## One axis, three stops

`free` / `hold` / `guard`, monotone in how much motion is allowed, surfaced as a
switch in the apex masthead and on `shell.html`.

The setting governs what may **replace** what you hold. It never governs what you
may **see**: every mode reaches every slot, every read is layered (rolling → held
→ network → navigate fallback), and an unparseable mode reads as `free`, so a
setting we cannot understand can never lock anybody down.

*Verified:* in `guard`, with the origin killed outright, the control page still
boots, reports both slots truthfully, and `/poll.html` still serves 200.

## Stillness

`install()` no longer calls `skipWaiting()`. A new worker waits until every tab
closes, the way firmware should sit still — confirmed in Chromium, where the
upgrade sat in `waiting` rather than seizing control. Skew between a held shell
and a newer worker is the **steady state**, not an error, so nothing gates on it
and an *old* worker is never treated as a foreign one. The holder pulls a waiting
worker forward from `shell.html` when they want it.

## Tests

`scripts/sw.test.mjs` grows the conversation's decisions into assertions, each
negative-tested to confirm it actually fails when violated:

- `install()` must not call `skipWaiting` (comments stripped first — the handler
  explains in prose that it deliberately does not, and a naive search matched
  that sentence).
- `activate()` must not call `caches.delete` directly; `retireLegacy()` must
  refuse while the floor is incomplete.
- Promotion must merge; `MODES` must be exactly free/hold/guard; the page must
  offer all three; `getMode()` must fall back to the most permissive.

`119/119` suites pass.

## Also

- The apex masthead gets the posture switch — the thing you can point people at
  next month. Hidden until a worker actually controls the page, because a switch
  wired to nothing is worse than no switch, and re-probed on `controllerchange`
  so it appears on a *first* visit rather than only the second.
- `docs/origin.md` records the two-slot change, the lockout it fixes, and the
  see/replace invariant.

